---
title: "LLMs-assisted development"
sidebar:
  order: 10
---

When you read this page, you may already using Large-Language Models (LLMs) to assist you write code. LLM-assisted development has increasingly being adopted across the software industries (e.g., [Linux kernel](https://docs.kernel.org/process/coding-assistants.html) and [others](https://cloud.google.com/transform/101-real-world-generative-ai-use-cases-from-industry-leaders)) and bioinformatics (see, for example, [this training materials](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/large-language-models-and-their-applications-bioinformatics/) and [this review paper](https://academic.oup.com/bib/article/26/4/bbaf357/8212018)). Software development has come a long way from writing assembly code, writing higher-level code and rely on compiler and linker to generate machine code, to the rapid adoption of LLMs through agentic coding tools. Just as the introduction of a higher-level programming language did not eliminate the need for understanding the fundamentals of software engineering and problem solving, LLM-assisted development is not a substitute of that either. Like any tool, it is effectiveness depends on the skill of the person using it.

NAHPU welcomes LLM usage when it helps a human contributor solve problems within NAHPU. We also encourage the responsible use of LLMs in the development process. As it is a useful tool, excessive LLM usages carry [environmental impact](https://news.mit.edu/2025/explained-generative-ai-environmental-impact-0117). This page serves as a guide for responsible use of LLMs to assist contributors. It is also to provide transparency in the conduct of NAHPU development for the community. We hope NAHPU can continue to provide high-quality natural history software, while serving as an inter-disciplinary training platform for natural history work.

{% aside type="caution" title="LLM-assisted development is not vibe coding" %}
[Merriam-Webster](https://www.merriam-webster.com/slang/vibe-coding) describes vibe coding as careless AI-assisted coding where a person tells AI what to build and may not understand how or why the code works. That is not acceptable for NAHPU. Vibe-coded contributions and pull requests submitted by AI agents or bot accounts will be rejected.
{% /aside %}

{% aside type="note" title="Feedback is welcome" %}
LLMs, agentic coding development, and generative AI in general are rapidly evolving technologies. We welcome any feedback on this page and the responsible use of LLMs in NAHPU development. Please submit an issue or pull request to improve this page.
{% /aside %}

## Use `AGENTS.md` as context

NAHPU includes an `AGENTS.md` file at the root of [the app](https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu) and [API](https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu_api) repositories. This file is the project brief for LLM coding tools. It explains how each repository is organized, which commands to run, where different types of logic should be placed, and which architectural boundaries must not be crossed. These boundaries are based on the experience of developing NAHPU four years (~100k lines of code) before we began using LLMs. They also reflect established best practices for Flutter, Dart, and Rust.

## Current `AGENTS.md`

This is an example of the current `AGENTS.md` file in the [app](https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu) repository. It is a living document and may change over time. Different repository has its own `AGENTS.md` file. Please check the latest version in the respective repository.

`AGENTS.md` is useful for human contributors and LLM agents, as it helps both to understand the project structure, conventions, and the development workflow. Many agents can read `AGENTS.md` automatically. If your tool does not, paste it into the model context before asking for code changes.

```md
# Repository Guidelines

## Project Structure & Module Organization

NAHPU is a Flutter app with Rust via Flutter Rust Bridge. Dart lives in `lib/`: screens in `lib/screens/`, services/providers in `lib/services/`, styling in `lib/styles/`, and generated bridge Dart in `lib/src/rust/`. Rust wrappers live in `rust/src/api/`; keep core logic upstream. Tests are in `test/` and `integration_test/`. Assets are under `assets/`; platforms are `android/`, `ios/`, `linux/`, `macos/`, `web/`, and `windows/`.

## Build, Test, and Development Commands

- `flutter analyze`: analyze.
- `flutter test`: test.
- `flutter pub run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs`: codegen.
- `flutter_rust_bridge_codegen generate`: bridge codegen.
- `cargo check`: check rust code in `rust/`.
- `cargo clippy`: Rust linting.

## Coding Style & Naming Conventions

Use 2-space Dart formatting; run `dart format lib test integration_test`. Follow `flutter_lints` and `custom_lint`. Name Dart files in `snake_case.dart` and classes/widgets in `PascalCase`.

## Rust Best Practices

Keep Rust code, comments, and docstrings to 100 characters per line; wrap long signatures cleanly. Follow `rustfmt` and Rust API Guidelines. Prefer `struct` plus `impl` over loose globals. In `impl` blocks, put `pub fn` methods first and private helpers last. Use `?`, pattern matching, references over clones, and no `.unwrap()` unless justified. Use `snake_case` for functions/variables, `PascalCase` for types/traits/enums, and `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for constants.

## Flutter Best Practices

Keep screens as thin UI wrappers under `lib/screens/`. Put business logic in `lib/services/` and reusable UI in `lib/screens/shared/`. Do not place UI in services or return widgets from helpers; create widget classes, no functions return widgets. In widget classes, only `@override` methods go before `build`; keep `build` near the top and helpers below. Prefer `const`, immutable models, and Riverpod over mutable globals. Keep `build` side-effect free; do IO, database, and bridge work in services/providers. Guard async UI updates with `context.mounted`. Use theme values.

## Testing Guidelines

Add focused `*_test.dart` files in `test/`. Prefer service tests for import/export, persistence, validation, and migrations; add widget tests for regressions. Run integration tests for startup, navigation, IO, or bridge changes.

## Agent-Specific Instructions

Preserve user changes and avoid unrelated refactors. Always write the product name as `NAHPU`. Agents must not create commits, branches, pushes, or pull requests; leave Git under user control. After bridge API edits, regenerate bindings and verify analysis plus `cargo check` and `cargo clippy`. Add assets to `pubspec.yaml` only when needed.
```

## What the file does

`AGENTS.md` gives different LLM agents the same operating rules:

- It points agents to the important directories.
- It tells agents which commands to verify Dart, Flutter, and Rust changes.
- It keeps UI logic, business logic, generated bridge files, and Rust wrappers
  in the right places.
- It reminds agents that NAHPU has generated code and bridge code that require
  regeneration.
- It prevents unsafe workflow actions such as committing, branching, pushing, or
  opening pull requests on behalf of the contributor.

This makes agent output more predictable across tools such as Codex, Claude Code, Cursor, Continue, Copilot Workspace, or other model-based coding systems. Even when a model does not support `AGENTS.md` as a special file, the text works as plain prompt context.

## Workflow

{% steps %}

1. Commit your changes to a branch before starting the agent.
2. Start the agent in the repository root: [NAHPU](https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu), [API](https://github.com/nahpu_api), or the [documentation](https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu-docs), depending on the task.

    {% aside type="tip"%}
    Some feature addition or bug fix may require changes in both the [app](https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu) and [API](https://github.com/nahpu_api) repositories. In that case, include both repositories in the agent context. Please familiarize yourself with the [NAHPU architecture](./architecture) before asking the agent to make changes.
    {% /aside %}

3. Start with a plan mode and make sure the agent has read `AGENTS.md`.

    This may seem obvious. Evaluating the plan thoroughly will save time and effort. If it does not make sense, ask it to revise the plan before generating code.

    {% aside type="caution" title="Important"%}
    Undersanding of the NAHPU architecture, project structure, and problem domain is paramount important for the agent to generate code that is maintainable and correct. Please reach out to the maintainers if you need help understanding the architecture or the problem domain.
    {% /aside %}

5. Keep the task narrow: one bug, one feature, or one docs update.
6. Be strategic on which model to use: larger models for planning, smaller models for code generation. While larger models may excel at both planning and code generation, they are slower, more expensive, and use more energy.

    {% aside type="note"%}

    Not every task requires a state-of-the-art model. Smaller models may be sufficient for both planning and code generation, depending on the complexity of the task. Likewise, some small code changes or refactoring tasks may be faster and easier to perform manually.

    {% /aside %}

    {% aside type="tip"%}
    Each model release often comes with multiple model tiers. For example, the [OpenAI GPT 5.6](https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-6/) has three tiers of models: `sol`, `terra`, and `luna`. Each of this tier has different capability settings: low, medium, high, extra high, and ultra. We recommend inspecting a third-party benchmark for each model and starting with the option that provides the best balance between performance, latency, and cost for the task.

    A model is not the only factor that determines the quality of the output. The result also depends on the agent [harness](https://parallel.ai/articles/what-is-an-agent-harness) and the tools you use. Always evaluate the model's output critically and adjust your approach as needed. Developing this judgment is an essential skill for responsible LLM-assisted software development. It is also a reminder that LLMs are tools to augment software engineering expertise, not replace it.

    {% /aside %}

7. Review every generated change following the checklist provided [below](#contributor-checklists-for-evaluating-agent-output).
9. Write the pull request yourself, including tests run and screenshots for UI
   changes.

{% /steps %}

## Contributor checklists for evaluating agent output

- Verify Dart files are in `snake_case.dart` and classes/widgets are in `PascalCase` and other rules in the `AGENTS.md`. Not every model follows the instructions.
- Verify that no untested code is committed.
- Ensure that the agent run `flutter analyze`, `flutter test`, `dart format` for Dart changes, and `cargo check` and `cargo clippy` for Rust changes.
- Test manually.
- Check changes in `table.drift`. Any change in it requires a schema migration. Do not commit untested migrations or export changes.
